&gt; Now spend 0.5x their salary on AI, and they become 5x productive compared to no AI.
&gt; 
&gt; Now spend 1.1x their salary on AI, and they become 20x productive compared to no AI.<p>This is what I am getting at. If this were true - and I&#x27;ve yet to see evidence it is, despite some anecdotal evidence (organizationally, IME, it&#x27;s about 2-3x for power users, and probably a net loss of productivity in certain areas) - why would their headcount be growing so much still YoY, pretty much doubling every year. If the AI is 10-20xing why such a big jump in headcount? Yes, the numbers are shiny, the growth is apparent, I would just expect with this AI narrative they&#x27;d be &quot;doing more with less&quot; as this thing improves every year.<p>&gt; The AI usage still needs a knowledge worker to direct it in ways that it can&#x27;t direct itself, so you still need the employees.<p>This is the story AI sells though, and Anthropic has participated in it heavily. Yet, their interview process requires hard skills and doesn&#x27;t let you use AI, so clearly, what you&#x27;re saying is correct, it just doesn&#x27;t really align in my head with the whole knowledge worker being replaced narrative.
